# S12.1 final review: shared mappings and effective systemd fragment

## Findings

A process can retain a shared read-only mapping created from a read-write file
descriptor and later upgrade it to writable after filesystem modes change. In
addition, a higher-priority full systemd unit can shadow the reviewed file
without appearing in `DropInPaths`.

## Resolution

The repository barrier now rejects every retained shared mapping below the
protected trees, even when its current permissions omit `w`. Linux regression
coverage creates such a mapping after closing the original descriptor.

Preflight and the immediate pre-start gate now require the effective systemd
`FragmentPath` to be the exact reviewed file in `/etc/systemd/system`, while
continuing to require empty `DropInPaths` and static enablement.
